# CLAUDE.md's "Learned constraints" section is explicitly append-only -
# both Stream A and Stream B add a new line there on effectively every
# session, almost always near the very end of the list. When both
# streams' PRs are in flight around the same time (routine, given how
# often this file gets touched - 45+ commits as of 2026-09-05), git's
# default line-based merge sees two different, fully-compatible
# insertions at the same location and reports a conflict, even though
# there is nothing to actually resolve - the append-only Learned
# Constraints format is a Git "union" merge by design (see also
# supabase/migrations/, an append-only directory that only sequential,
# non-conflicting NEW filenames ever touch and so has never needed this).
#
# `merge=union` tells Git to auto-resolve a conflict in this file by
# taking BOTH sides' added lines rather than stopping for a human to
# choose - mechanizing exactly what's already been done by hand on every
# recent PR ("I manually resolve them by accepting both changes").
#
# Known tradeoff, accepted deliberately (2026-09-05, confirmed with
# Ferenz): this applies to the WHOLE file, not just the Learned
# Constraints section - if both streams ever genuinely REWRITE the same
# existing line elsewhere in CLAUDE.md at the same time (e.g. both reword
# the same Hard Invariant), a union merge would silently combine both
# edits into a garbled result instead of surfacing a real conflict for a
# human to catch. Every CLAUDE.md conflict on record so far has been a
# pure append to Learned Constraints, never a simultaneous edit to
# existing prose elsewhere - if that ever changes, revisit whether this
# section needs to move to its own file instead.
CLAUDE.md merge=union
